<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Werle</h1><p class="st-meta">1234 CE – 1436 CE · capital Güstrow</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Mecklenburg lordship of the Niklotid princes centred on Güstrow, who styled themselves princes of the Wends until the line died out in 1436.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the division of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-mecklenburg">Mecklenburg</a> among the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-borwin-ii-lord-of-mecklenburg">Henry Borwin II</a> in 1229, Nicholas I received the eastern lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustrow">Güstrow</a> and the castle of Werle. His line ruled as lords of Werle, alongside their cousins in Mecklenburg, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-rostock">Rostock</a> and Parchim-Richenberg, all descended from the Obotrite prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niklot">Niklot</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The lords ruled a land of lakes, farmland and small towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustrow">Güstrow</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waren-muritz">Waren</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malchow">Malchow</a>, which grew with German settlement. They repeatedly split the territory among branches such as Werle-Güstrow and Werle-Goldberg. From the 15th century they used the title Prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wends">Wends</a>, recalling their Slavic ancestry, while their power slowly waned beside the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-mecklenburg">Mecklenburg</a> dukes.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last lord, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-werle">William of Werle</a>, died in 1436 without a son, and his lands passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg">Duchy of Mecklenburg</a>. The dukes adopted the title Prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wends">Wends</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustrow">Güstrow</a> later became a ducal residence.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lordship-of-werle.jpg" alt="Lordship of Werle" width="320" height="252"><figcaption>Image: Alphathon /ˈæɫfə.θɒn/ (talk) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lordship_of_Werle_locator_map_(1250).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1234 CE – 1436 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Güstrow</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nicholas I of Werle, William of Werle</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Werle, Principality of Wenden</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1335&amp;focus=lordship-of-werle" aria-label="Lordship of Werle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4950 330 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Werle lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5270 586L5254 584M5270 586L5254 591"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5254 584h0M5254 591h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5270 586h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5270 586h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Güstrow. <a href="/atlas?year=1335&amp;focus=lordship-of-werle">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
